ncluding those outside the map or with a very uncertain GPS rate (marked *), the difference is 1.7 mm a year. Some GPS records ended before the radar record began in 2016, so part of the difference is the two instruments measuring different years."},"nisar":{"ok":true,"summary":"NISAR's L-band radar passed over Miami 26 times between November 2025 and October 2026. Adding its 25 consecutive interferograms gives the ground motion over those 324 days. Over that time the long-term rates predict that neighborhoods should differ from each other by only about 1.3 mm. NISAR's neighborhood values scatter by about 6 mm. That scatter is the atmosphere and ionosphere delaying the radar signal, not the ground moving, so NISAR cannot yet confirm or contradict the long-term rates. It needs a longer record before the noise averages down to millimeters. In a typical 12-day pair NISAR returned a measurement over 95% of the land in these neighborhoods, and the Sentinel-1 record gives a reliable long-term rate over 95%. Each figure uses its own product's quality flags, so the two are about level here: in a built-up city both radars see most of the ground.","product":"NISAR L2 GUNW (provisional)","doi":"10.5067/NIL2GUNW-P1","track_frame":"170_D_075","band":"L","polarization":"HH","first_date":"20251122","last_date":"20261012","days":324,"n_pairs":25,"neighborhood_scatter_mm":6.0,"expected_range_mm":1.31,"correlation":-0.14,"land_measured_typical_pair":0.952,"land_measured_every_pair":0.095,"opera_land_measured":0.948,"n_neighborhoods_compared":49,"pairs":[{"reference":"20251122","secondary":"20251204","granule":"NISAR_L2_PR_GUNW_005_170_D_075_006_4000_SH_20251122T000440_20251122T000515_20251204T000441_20251204T000515_P05023_N_F_J_001","land_measured":0.963,"ionosphere_screen_rejected":0.0,"scatter_mm":12.0},{"reference":"20251204","secondary":"20251216","granule":"NISAR_L2_PR_GUNW_006_170_D_075_007_4000_SH_20251204T000441_20251204T000515_20251216T000441_20251216T000516_P05023_N_F_J_001